# SafeVisionAI

## AI-Based Real-Time Worker Safety Monitoring System

SafeVisionAI is a computer vision-based worker safety monitoring system designed to detect PPE compliance violations from video streams or camera feeds.

The system uses YOLO-based object detection to identify workers and safety equipment, records detected violations, and provides a dashboard for reviewing violation events with screenshots.

## Features

* PPE detection using YOLO
* Worker safety rule monitoring
* Video file testing
* Webcam/camera input support
* IP/network camera or video source support
* Violation detection and logging
* Automatic screenshot capture for violations
* Violation records displayed on a Streamlit dashboard
* Employee details management
* Real-time monitoring support

## Project Workflow

The system follows this general workflow:

```text
Video / Camera Source
        ↓
Video Processing
        ↓
YOLO Object Detection
        ↓
Safety Rule Checking
        ↓
Violation Detection
        ↓
Violation Logging
        ↓
Screenshot Capture
        ↓
Streamlit Dashboard
```

## Project Setup

### 1. Clone the Repository

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd SafeVisionAI
```

### 2. Create a Virtual Environment

It is recommended to use a virtual environment for the project.

```bash
python -m venv .venv
```

Activate it on Windows:

```bash
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

### 3. Install Requirements

Install the required Python libraries:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

The main dependencies include:

* Ultralytics
* OpenCV
* NumPy
* Pandas
* Streamlit
* Pillow

## Model Configuration

Before running the detection pipeline, open:

```text
test_pipeline.py
```

Inside this file, enter the path to your trained YOLO model.

Example:

```python
MODEL_PATH = "models/best.pt"
```

You also need to specify the video source.

### Using a Demo Video

Enter the path to your video file:

```python
VIDEO_SOURCE = "demo_video.mp4"
```

You can also provide the full path:

```python
VIDEO_SOURCE = r"C:\path\to\demo_video.mp4"
```

### Using a Camera

To use the default camera/webcam:

```python
VIDEO_SOURCE = 0
```

For another camera index, you can use a different number such as:

```python
VIDEO_SOURCE = 1
```

### Using a Video/Camera URL

If your implementation supports a network video stream, the source can be provided as a URL:

```python
VIDEO_SOURCE = "YOUR_VIDEO_SOURCE_URL"
```

## Employee Details

Create a folder named:

```text
pages
```

Inside the `pages` folder, place:

```text
employee_details.py
```

The structure should look like:

```text
SafeVisionAI/
│
├── dashboard.py
├── test_pipeline.py
├── requirements.txt
├── README.md
│
├── pages/
│   └── employee_details.py
│
└── models/
    └── best.pt
```

## Running the Dashboard

Run the Streamlit dashboard using:

```bash
streamlit run dashboard.py
```

After starting Streamlit, open the URL provided in the terminal/browser.

The dashboard allows you to review recorded safety violations and their associated screenshots.

## Testing the Model

After configuring the model path and video source in `test_pipeline.py`, run:

```bash
python test_pipeline.py
```

The pipeline will process the selected video or camera source and detect safety-rule violations.

Detected violations will be recorded by the system.

## Violation Logging

When a person is detected as violating a safety rule, the violation is logged.

The system records the violation along with a screenshot/evidence image.

These recorded violations can then be viewed through the Streamlit dashboard.

The general flow is:

```text
Person detected
       ↓
PPE/Safety rule checked
       ↓
Violation detected
       ↓
Violation recorded
       ↓
Screenshot captured
       ↓
Displayed on dashboard
```

